# Configuration compliance, not Workstation Assurance (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-09-27):

> **Fix terminology on WA through documents. Use industry standard
> terminology seen in CMMC and other regulatory frameworks to
> describe/name what WA does. WA is not an industry term.**

Earlier the same day, on the same subject:

> **Agree on removing WA branding universally as a hard rule.**

## The rule

> **The capability historically branded Workstation Assurance is named
> configuration compliance. Describe it in the vocabulary the frameworks
> use: baseline configuration, security configuration settings,
> configuration drift, system component inventory. Do not use "Workstation
> Assurance" or "WA" as a product brand in new work. Do not use "endpoint
> posture" either; it is a vendor phrase that appears in no framework.
> Shipped identifiers keep their names.**

## 1. What the capability is, in framework words

It checks systems against an approved configuration baseline and reports
deviations. The frameworks name that precisely:

| Framework | Identifier | Wording |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CMMC Level 2 | **CM.L2-3.4.1** | System Baselining. Establish and maintain baseline configurations and inventories of organizational systems, including hardware, software, firmware, and documentation |
| CMMC Level 2 | **CM.L2-3.4.2** | Security Configuration Enforcement. Establish and enforce security configuration settings; monitor, detect, and remediate configuration deviations |
| NIST SP 800-53 | **CM-2** | Baseline Configuration |
| NIST SP 800-53 | **CM-6** | Configuration Settings |
| NIST SP 800-53 | **CM-8** | System Component Inventory |
| NIST SP 800-53 | **CA-7** | Continuous Monitoring |
| Supporting | SP 800-128, SP 800-70 | Security-focused configuration management; configuration checklists |

Ecosystem sources a reader will recognize: Center for Internet Security
(CIS) Benchmarks, and Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security
Technical Implementation Guides (STIGs).

## 2. Required vocabulary

| Use | For |
| --- | --- |
| **configuration compliance** | The capability and the operator-facing function |
| **security configuration assessment** | The act of checking a system |
| **configuration drift** | What a failed check found |
| **baseline configuration** | The approved reference state |
| **security configuration settings** | The individual parameters |
| **system component inventory** | The asset side, and the right phrase at CMMC Level 1 |
| **host**, **endpoint**, **system component** | The thing being assessed |

"Configuration compliance" is both regulatory and a recognized commercial
category, so it satisfies
[`competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc`](competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc)
at the same time. That is why it wins over the alternatives.

**CMMC Level 1 nuance.** Level 1 comes from Federal Acquisition Regulation
clause 52.204-21 and contains **no** configuration-baseline practice. Do
not imply CM.L2-3.4.1 or CM.L2-3.4.2 coverage in Level 1 copy. At Level 1
speak about **system component inventory** and basic safeguarding instead.

## 3. Rejected alternatives, with reasons

| Rejected | Why |
| --- | --- |
| **Workstation Assurance / WA** | Not an industry term, and it understates the scope: the capability covers servers, workstations, appliances, and network endpoints |
| **endpoint posture** | A vendor phrase. It appears in no CMMC practice, no NIST SP 800-171 requirement, and no NIST SP 800-53 control title. It fails the operator instruction to use framework vocabulary |
| **System Assurance / SA** | **SA is the NIST SP 800-53 System and Services Acquisition control family.** This product speaks 800-53 family names, so the acronym collides in exactly the vocabulary we use |

## 4. Shipped identifiers keep their names

This is a branding retirement, not an identifier migration. Renaming a
registered service is the specific failure
[`service-registration-hygiene.mdc`](service-registration-hygiene.mdc)
exists to prevent, and it caused a real multi-day shutdown hang on the lab
host.

**Never rename:**

- `aic-workstation-assurance-agent-service` (canonical service name)
- `wa_internal` and `wa_external` license entitlements
- `wa_seat` provisioning action, `provision_wa.rs`
- `AIC-WA-INT` and `AIC-WA-EXT` Stock Keeping Unit codes on price papers.
  A SKU code is a billing identifier; changing it is a commercial change,
  not a terminology fix
- `Build-PimWorkstationAssuranceAgentMsi.ps1` and sibling script names
- Existing database columns, table prefixes, and route paths
- The WA token path

**Product display names** (for example the installer name shown in Add or
Remove Programs) are customer-visible branding, so they may change, but
only as a deliberate packaging change made with the installer work, never
as a side effect of editing documentation. If changed, the name is title
case: **AIC Configuration Compliance Agent**.

## 5. Do not sweep with a blind find-replace

A mechanical substitution breaks prose and identifiers. Read each hit and
rewrite the sentence.

| Hazard | What goes wrong |
| --- | --- |
| Product display names | `**AIC Workstation Assurance**` becomes `**AIC endpoint posture**`, which is broken English and renames a shipped product |
| Headings next to identifiers | A heading changes while the adjacent script and service names correctly do not, leaving the document contradicting itself |
| WA used as a noun | "plus WA, binder, and attest" becomes "plus endpoint posture, binder, and attest", which does not read |
| SKU tables | A SKU description changes while the SKU code does not, so the row no longer matches the code |
| Renders | Editing markdown makes its Word and PDF twins stale; re-render them in the same change set ([`docs-render-docx-pdf.mdc`](docs-render-docx-pdf.mdc)) |
| Price papers | Changing customer-facing price copy additionally requires a revision-date bump and a re-render ([`gtm-price-list-revision-date.mdc`](gtm-price-list-revision-date.mdc)) |

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation**, observed in the working tree on 2026-09-27 during a
find-replace sweep:

```text
| 4 | **Endpoint posture** + CLI + Tauri | Build-PimWorkstationAssuranceAgentMsi.ps1 |
    aic-workstation-assurance-agent-service | **AIC endpoint posture** |
```

Three defects in one row: a vendor term instead of framework vocabulary, a
renamed product display name, and a display name that is not even
grammatical English beside a script and service that kept their names.

**Fix:**

```text
| 4 | **Configuration compliance** + CLI + Tauri | Build-PimWorkstationAssuranceAgentMsi.ps1 |
    aic-workstation-assurance-agent-service | **AIC Configuration Compliance Agent** |
```

The function is named in framework vocabulary, the script and service keep
their identifiers, and the display name is title case and reads correctly.
The display-name change lands with the installer work, not with the
documentation edit.

## Required agent behavior

1. In new prose, write configuration compliance and the section 2
   vocabulary. Anchor claims to CM.L2-3.4.1, CM.L2-3.4.2, CM-2, CM-6,
   CM-8, or CA-7 where a control reference helps.
2. Never introduce "Workstation Assurance", "WA", "endpoint posture", or
   "System Assurance" as a product brand in new work.
3. When correcting existing documents, read each occurrence. Rewrite the
   sentence rather than substituting a token, and leave every identifier in
   section 4 alone.
4. Re-render Word and PDF twins for any markdown changed, and bump the
   revision date on customer-facing price papers.
5. Cursor rules, `docs/dev`, and commit bodies may reference the historical
   name for traceability.
6. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change set.
   The workspace `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

